{"lines":[{"y":0.065121168797061535,"w":0.081082706451415909,"h":0.022373941277812892,"x":0.77618797005575757,"c":1,"t":"Reading"},{"y":0.11632662390518589,"t":"READING PASSAGE 3","w":0.29552294413248698,"x":0.069367041692416323,"c":1,"h":0.021269995400157948},{"y":0.15406976712464082,"w":0.78195490519205735,"c":1,"h":0.021802326209761524,"t":"You should spend about 20 minutes on Questions 27-40, which are based on Reading","x":0.069548863110193607},{"x":0.069548872672526019,"t":"Passage 3 below.","w":0.16353383382161457,"c":1,"y":0.17289117856618952,"h":0.020422774595517335},{"h":0.021802326209761635,"t":"Book Review","x":0.38157894650958452,"w":0.1729323323567708,"c":1,"y":0.21366279020180523},{"y":0.25434642605398361,"h":0.020362876110973427,"t":"The Happiness Industry: How the Government and Big Business","w":0.57894734700520833,"c":1,"x":0.1785714391801444},{"y":0.27046037663788569,"w":0.1790391286214193,"c":1,"t":"Sold Us Well-Being","h":0.02183725291962646,"x":0.37948708733941355},{"c":1,"h":0.020847671813375435,"w":0.16589328765869138,"x":0.38693833720540804,"y":0.30821948869242921,"t":"By William Davies"},{"h":0.019940651391908149,"x":0.069515765962576623,"w":0.69182286580403651,"c":1,"t":"'Happiness is the ultimate goal because it is self-evidently good. If we are asked why","y":0.34841397108241279},{"h":0.024288204509983147,"t":"happiness matters we can give no further external reason. It just obviously does matter.' This","x":0.069444643007759807,"y":0.36451935941869928,"w":0.75400146484375008,"c":1},{"t":"pronouncement by Richard Layard, an economist and advocate of 'positive psychology',","x":0.071323333445764095,"w":0.7238473002115885,"y":0.38453710418564491,"h":0.021980624619561961,"c":1},{"x":0.071352447354048915,"c":1,"y":0.40496987029921572,"t":"summarises the beliefs of many people today. For Layard and others like him, it is obvious that","h":0.020628482698242911,"w":0.77271987915039064},{"h":0.021247755690536052,"c":1,"x":0.071363927710573691,"w":0.7613936869303386,"y":0.42242077339673889,"t":"the purpose of government is to promote a state of collective well-being. The only question is"},{"t":"how to achieve it, and here positive psychology - a supposed science that not only identifies","y":0.44173123201054987,"x":0.071319923952652756,"w":0.750218505859375,"h":0.022211744181970139,"c":1},{"y":0.46160946771324507,"t":"what makes people happy but also allows their happiness to be measured - can show the way.","x":0.073230662958958984,"w":0.76329767862955733,"c":1,"h":0.020187845862610243},{"t":"Equipped with this science, they say, governments can secure happiness in society in a way they","x":0.07330827709483051,"w":0.78007517496744794,"h":0.018995492594977459,"y":0.48100450738807266,"c":1},{"y":0.50289761780084075,"h":0.016097875080489321,"c":1,"w":0.19736841837565106,"x":0.071428572733609405,"t":"never could in the past."},{"x":0.071342401370838124,"y":0.53681412208470358,"w":0.72010518391927081,"h":0.0205716104255671,"t":"It is an astonishingly crude and simple-minded way of thinking, and for that very reason","c":1},{"w":0.7221751912434895,"c":1,"y":0.55346866581590637,"x":0.071267913205303735,"h":0.024663113350263388,"t":"increasingly popular. Those who think in this way are oblivious to the vast philosophical"},{"t":"literature in which the meaning and value of happiness have been explored and questioned,","h":0.02083126100047672,"x":0.073233570097313627,"y":0.57441426207345236,"w":0.74635894775390632,"c":1},{"x":0.073157267468857298,"y":0.59214634946031375,"c":1,"w":0.77285380045572916,"h":0.023163335521376172,"t":"and write as if nothing of any importance had been thought on the subject until it came to their"},{"w":0.78036849975585942,"h":0.023177845126494123,"y":0.61132730737818575,"t":"attention. It was the philosopher Jeremy Bentham (1748-1832) who was more than anyone else","x":0.073162723757693199,"c":1},{"w":0.75972844441731768,"t":"responsible for the development of this way of thinking. For Bentham it was obvious that the","x":0.073146663202026244,"y":0.62997762538367397,"h":0.023988787619743657,"c":1},{"x":0.075070955786568638,"y":0.64860469645823593,"h":0.023212423035964824,"c":1,"w":0.77652404785156248,"t":"human good consists of pleasure and the absence of pain. The Greek philosopher Aristotle may"},{"t":"have identified happiness with self-realisation in the 4th century BC, and thinkers throughout the","y":0.66699808977091701,"w":0.78984491984049487,"h":0.025630958296756767,"x":0.075007037598527587,"c":1},{"x":0.075062220264213025,"y":0.68862832732990709,"t":"ages may have struggled to reconcile the pursuit of happiness with other human values, but for","c":1,"w":0.77470270792643225,"h":0.022625719894536989},{"t":"Bentham all this was mere metaphysics or fiction. Without knowing anything much of him or the","y":0.70646429185979376,"x":0.076925665393673348,"w":0.78979278564453126,"h":0.024543735187282478,"c":1},{"t":"school of moral theory he established - since they are by education and intellectual conviction","x":0.075018078362667773,"y":0.72521273495282901,"h":0.026529694094783784,"w":0.77103830973307286,"c":1},{"h":0.026251443493235826,"y":0.74400633566691887,"w":0.75790100097656243,"t":"illiterate in the history of ideas - our advocates of positive psychology follow in his tracks in","x":0.076864002139690898,"c":1},{"y":0.76372916488464371,"t":"rejecting as outmoded and irrelevant pretty much the entirety of ethical reflection on human","x":0.076897550335175993,"w":0.74847991943359371,"h":0.025065317049536184,"c":1},{"x":0.077067666999539555,"c":1,"t":"happiness to date.","y":0.78911783664596225,"w":0.15225564320882162,"h":0.017707662588538287},{"y":0.81933618022101995,"h":0.028167682851581333,"x":0.078757614236665177,"c":1,"t":"But as William Davies notes in his recent book The Happiness Industry, the view that happiness","w":0.78427886962890625},{"y":0.83857280463099371,"x":0.07688021344094087,"w":0.76368209838867185,"h":0.028397813736816713,"c":1,"t":"is the only self-evident good is actually a way of limiting moral inquiry. One of the virtues of"},{"y":0.85850427910032623,"w":0.78050562540690116,"c":1,"t":"this rich, lucid and arresting book is that it places the current cult of happiness in a well-defined","h":0.026715464693303992,"x":0.078768807681538627},{"t":"historical framework. Rightly, Davies begins his story with Bentham, noting that he was far","y":0.87767349717284959,"w":0.75044789632161457,"h":0.027414288738813242,"c":1,"x":0.078761674339819579},{"w":0.77101786295572916,"y":0.89777742982526421,"h":0.024361911928431179,"t":"more than a philosopher. Davies writes, 'Bentham's activities were those which we might now","c":1,"x":0.080672757962982469},{"x":0.080613075731496342,"h":0.028200321479988855,"t":"associate with a public sector management consultant'. In the 1790s, he wrote to the Home Office","w":0.79934117635091151,"y":0.91520011758065745,"c":1},{"y":0.93499488939939523,"t":"suggesting that the departments of government be linked together through a set of'conversation","h":0.030449959975400454,"x":0.080604999986828046,"w":0.78435953776041656,"c":1},{"w":0.72966420491536454,"h":0.026970639201186919,"t":"tubeẾnd to the Bank of England with a design for a printing device that could produce","c":1,"x":0.080682720796753621,"y":0.95613027932087702}],"h":3106,"w":2400}